Looking at this problem, I need to prove that `K.Total(s + t) == K.Total(s) + K.Total(t)` using induction on `|s|`.

Let me analyze what's available:
- `End.Base(s, t)`: requires `|s| == 0`, ensures the goal
- `End.Direct(s, t)`: requires `|s| > 0` and `K.Total(s[1..] + t) == K.Total(s[1..]) + K.Total(t)`, ensures the goal
- `Prove(s, t)`: the recursive call I can make

The proof strategy is:
1. Base case: when `|s| == 0`, call `End.Base(s, t)`
2. Inductive case: when `|s| > 0`:
   - Recursively call `Prove(s[1..], t)` to get `K.Total(s[1..] + t) == K.Total(s[1..]) + K.Total(t)`
   - Then call `End.Direct(s, t)` which uses that fact to prove the goal

Let me verify the decreases clause: `Prove` decreases on `|s|`, and `s[1..]` has length `|s| - 1 < |s|` when `|s| > 0`, so the recursive call terminates.

